  "why": "The hollow-block ribbed slab (very common in Egypt) reduces concrete by filling between ribs with blocks, so it carries a distinct block-laying trade on top of formwork, steel and concrete; each has its own crew and the net concrete volume is far lower per m2 than a solid slab, which the decomposition makes explicit.",
  "assumptions": [
    "Net concrete ≈0.10 m3/m2 (ribs + topping)",
    "Single 8 hr/day shift",
    "Concrete/clay hollow blocks supplied to level",
    "Full soffit form (blocks bear on the ply)",
    "Rib spacing ~500 mm c/c, topping ~50–60 mm"
  ],
  "exclusions": [
    "Abnormal congestion",
    "Rework/broken block replacement beyond wastage",
    "Solid heads/beam strips priced separately where dense",
    "Finishes and screed"
